  (commentary generated by claude-fable-5)
Energy is the morning's story: Brent and WTI are down roughly 5% and 4% to $84 and $79 as the Middle East war premium deflates — the US says it intercepted an Iranian attack on military bases in what reports suggest is the closing salvo of the conflict, and after last week's Iran-driven spike the market is rushing to reprice supply risk back out. Henry Hub followed, off nearly 4%. Elsewhere the risk tone is sharply bifurcated: the KOSPI cratered almost 11% and the Nikkei 4% after a profit miss and aggressive capex signals from the Korean memory-chip sector reignited AI-oversupply and cost fears, dragging Nasdaq futures down ~1% even as the Dow gained 1% and the FTSE hit a record — a rotation out of the AI complex into banks, energy and value rather than an outright risk-off.

The other axis is today's FOMC — arguably the most up-for-grabs decision in memory, with Chair Warsh giving markets little to anchor on and even a hike not fully ruled out, per reports. Treasuries are nonetheless bid into the meeting, the curve bull-flattening with the long bond up 0.45%, while the fading war premium is bleeding into precious metals: gold -0.9%, silver -2%, aluminium -1.5%. In ags, arabica surged 4.6% to fresh records on the US–Colombia tariff spat, wheat climbed a second day on faltering Black Sea exports amid escalating Russia–Ukraine strikes, and cattle extended their squeeze.

Watch the Fed statement and presser, then mega-cap tech earnings after the close for the next cue on index futures.

